      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.094,
            0.875,
            0.141
          ],
          "content": "**Take out the plug from the key. Remove the ammeter and voltmeter from the circuit.** Insert the ammeter in series with the resistor $R _ { \\jmath }$ **as shown in Fig. 11.11. Note the ammeter** reading, $I _ { \\iota } .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.212,
            0.153,
            0.698,
            0.259
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.427,
                0.276,
                0.546,
                0.291
              ],
              "content": "Figure 11.11"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_1.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.296,
            0.873,
            0.329
          ],
          "content": "Similarly, measure the currents through $R _ { _ 2 }$ and $R _ { _ { 3 ^ { \\prime } } }$ Let these be $I _ { z }$ and $I _ { \\mathfrak { z } } ,$ **respectively.** What is the relationship between $I , \\; I _ { 1 } , \\; I _ { 2 }$ and $I _ { 3 } ?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.276,
            0.35,
            0.898,
            0.384
          ],
          "content": "**It is observed that the total current I, is equal to the sum of the separate currents through each branch of the combination.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.308,
            0.386,
            0.897,
            0.405
          ],
          "content": "I = I_{_1} + I_{_2} + I_{_3}\\tag{11.15}",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.275,
            0.406,
            0.9,
            0.455
          ],
          "content": "Let $R _ { p }$ **be the equivalent resistance of the parallel combination of resistors. By applying Ohm’s law to the parallel combination of resistors, we have**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.308,
            0.458,
            0.896,
            0.477
          ],
          "content": "\\boldsymbol { \\mathit { \\Pi } } = \\boldsymbol { V } / R _ { p }\\tag{11.16}",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.307,
            0.478,
            0.737,
            0.495
          ],
          "content": "**On applying Ohm’s law to each resistor, we have**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.307,
            0.497,
            0.896,
            0.517
          ],
          "content": "I_{1}=V/R_{1}; \\quad I_{2}=V/R_{2}; \\quad  and   I_{3}=V/R_{3}\\tag{11.17}",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.307,
            0.518,
            0.644,
            0.535
          ],
          "content": "**From Eqs. (11.15) to (11.17), we have**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.411,
            0.537,
            0.644,
            0.558
          ],
          "content": "V/R_{p}=V/R_{1}+V/R_{2}+V/R_{3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.308,
            0.556,
            0.334,
            0.569
          ],
          "content": "**or**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.414,
            0.569,
            0.896,
            0.589
          ],
          "content": "1 / R_{p}=1 / R_{l}+1 / R_{2}+1 / R_{3}\\tag{11.18}",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.275,
            0.593,
            0.899,
            0.643
          ],
          "content": "**Thus, we may conclude that the reciprocal of the equivalent resistance of a group of resistances joined in parallel is equal to the sum of the reciprocals of the individual resistances.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.448,
            0.97,
            0.552,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.307,
            0.653,
            0.454,
            0.668
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Example 11.8"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.306,
            0.668,
            0.899,
            0.749
          ],
          "content": "In the circuit diagram given in Fig. 11.10, suppose the resistors $R _ { _ I } ,$ $R _ { 2 }$ and $R _ { 3 }$ **have the values 5** Ω**, 10** Ω**, 30** Ω**, respectively, which have been connected to a battery of 12 V. Calculate (a) the current through each resistor, (b) the total current in the circuit, and (c) the total circuit resistance.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.308,
            0.757,
            0.399,
            0.771
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Solution"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.307,
            0.772,
            0.619,
            0.789
          ],
          "content": "R_{1}=5\\ \\Omega, R_{2}=10\\ \\Omega, \\mathrm{and} R_{3}=30\\ \\Omega.",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_19.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.307,
            0.789,
            0.731,
            0.805
          ],
          "content": "Potential difference across the battery, $V   = 1 2   \\mathrm { V } .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.307,
            0.805,
            0.899,
            0.852
          ],
          "content": "**This is also the potential difference across each of the individual resistor; therefore, to calculate the current in the resistors, we use Ohm’s law.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.307,
            0.853,
            0.644,
            0.887
          ],
          "content": "**The current** $I_{l}, \\mathrm{through} R_{l} = \\mathrm{V/R}_{l}$ **I** = 12 V/5 Ω = **2.4 A.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.09,
            0.917,
            0.124,
            0.931
          ],
          "content": "186"
        },
